text:
lee: Quick heads-up — ran into an annoying telemetry parity edgecase in the onboarding sampleapps. When an auth-refresh retry path runs in our Node quickstart the x-request-id disappears on the retried outbound HTTP call. Minimal repro below.

anya: that will break log->trace joins. Is it specific to our fetch retry pattern or more general?

rob: Could be two things: (1) the retry lib clones the Request/Config without copying custom headers, or (2) our OTEL outgoing instrumentation is attached before the retry wrapper mutates the request.

lee: minimal repro (Node):
```js
// repro.js
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const fetchRetry = require('fetch-retry')(fetch);
await fetchRetry('https://internal/generate', { retries: 1, headers: { 'x-request-id': 'rid-edge-1' } });
```

lee: trimmed logs: 
2026-07-14T11:22:01Z sampleapp INFO incoming req id=rid-edge-1 route=/generate
2026-07-14T11:22:01Z sampleapp DEBUG http.outgoing host=internal x-request-id=rid-edge-1
2026-07-14T11:22:01Z sampleapp WARN http.retry status=401 auth-refresh
2026-07-14T11:22:02Z sampleapp DEBUG http.outgoing host=internal x-request-id=-
2026-07-14T11:22:02Z sampleapp INFO request complete route=/generate req_id=-

maria: that dash is our logger redaction showing a missing value, not masked PII. We should be able to detect missing vs redacted in dashboards.

rob: mitigation options: (A) change quickstart recommendation away from fetch-retry to a pattern that preserves headers; (B) add a tiny wrapper in sampleapp that ensures headers are copied into retry requests; (C) make OTEL/http hooks re-inject allowlisted headers from a per-request context store (ALS/ContextVar/Context) on every outgoing attempt. I prefer (C) for cross-library coverage.

anya: agree — reinjection via a context store is the least surprising for users. But we must be careful not to re-inject sensitive headers like Authorization. Add an allowlist (x-request-id, traceparent, baggage) and never copy Authorization.

lee: I prototyped a small middleware using AsyncLocalStorage that seeds requestId from incoming headers; the otel http hook then does: headers['x-request-id'] = headers['x-request-id'] || als.getStore()?.requestId. Works in the repro.

rob: can you paste the middleware snippet? and add a tiny e2e that simulates 401->refresh->retry? We'll need that as a regression test.

lee: middleware snippet:
```js
// seed request id
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const id = req.headers['x-request-id'] || `rid-${Math.floor(Math.random()*1e6)}`;
  asyncLocalStorage.run({ requestId: id }, () => next());
});
// otel hook: when instrumenting outgoing HTTP
const store = asyncLocalStorage.getStore();
if (store && !headers['x-request-id']) headers['x-request-id'] = store.requestId;
```

maria: nice. Two notes: (1) add allowlist check so we never touch Authorization or cookie headers, (2) update the logging/redaction formatter so missing/null is distinct from redacted values in JSON logs (e.g. add a field redaction_status: "missing" vs "masked"). That will help dashboard owners triage.

anya: also flag this for SDK parity — run the same repro in Python (requests + urllib3 Retry) and Go (net/http redirect/retry) sampleapps. If they fail similarly we should add the same lightweight helper in each quickstart.

rob: action items: @lee open a PR with ALS middleware + e2e that forces a 401 refresh and asserts x-request-id preserved; @maria open a small logging-redaction tweak PR to surface missing vs masked in JSON; @anya run quick parity smoke in Go/Python and post results. I'll review the OTEL hook change. :thumbsup:

telemetry-bot: Created issue #5124: "sampleapps: x-request-id lost across auth/retry flows; add context reinjection + clarify redaction semantics" (assigned devex).

lee: one more practical note: until the fix lands I'll add a short comment in quickstart README warning about header loss with some retry libs. That should reduce onboarding confusion.

anya: thanks — keep us posted. If parity check finds issues in Go/Python we'll coordinate a cross-SDK pattern.

maria: marking as P0 for DX onboarding because it breaks debug/telemetry for new users.

lee: filing PR now.
